math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503189} {:answer/id #uuid "6d39b890-a8bb-5142-8e49-f107cae1876d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503200} {:answer/id #uuid "8c54ec07-80f9-5923-810a-138566a95956" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503264} {:answer/id #uuid "dd688757-abed-5cc0-9a5c-d65b611ef9fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e6a7937-0a34-5d46-ab37-98c9e47880b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503016} :answer-field/id #uuid "abd0e436-6a81-5d55-beca-423ce27fb414" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "74b05c82-78aa-5425-a974-d8adb06f503c" :question/math-academy-id "q-337372" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y={e}^{-5x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-2x})}^{′}+2(u{e}^{-2x}) & ={e}^{-5x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-2x}-2u{e}^{-2x}+2u{e}^{-2x} & ={e}^{-5x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-2x}-\\cancel{2u{e}^{-2x}}+\\cancel{2u{e}^{-2x}} & ={e}^{-5x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & ={e}^{-5x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-2x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-2x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x}\\cdot {e}^{-2x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397006 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157456 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ac34a18-053d-5eab-8c2c-bd0f15658186" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c0ef841-3242-5caa-a95a-63ce8b7efbf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397008} {:answer/id #uuid "5c1855a8-8668-5bad-a68f-