elect} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397019}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a01f9794-773d-5e12-a888-6dc684788a68" :question/math-academy-id "q-337376" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=5.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{3x})}^{′}-3(u{e}^{3x}) & =5 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x}-3u{e}^{3x} & =5 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}}-\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}} & =5 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x} & =5 \\\\ {u}^{′} & =5{e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =5{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 5{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =-\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{3x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{3x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{-3x}\\cdot {e}^{3x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503742 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157456 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ac34a18-053d-5eab-8c2c-bd0f15658186" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1713da84-5fd1-5d91-a845-7d3a35da3e54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503029} {:answer/id #uuid "320352c9-552c-5248-98c4-9b92c0952e80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503072} {:answer/id #uuid "5389ae9c-5911-5ab4-91a8-03ab68acf3d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503141} {:answer/id #uuid "b032a7d0-7e3b-558c-b11b-cca85f9404d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503338} {:answer/id #uuid "e65ebc70-53a8-5cc6-b026-dc7951854da4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b032a7d0-7e3b-558c-b11b-cca85f9404d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503338} :answer-field/id #uuid "38566c9c-eb99-5a6b-a9ad-b3ded30c9daa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503537} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01e6512f-2f3d-5c8c-92fc-e571cc206291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int {e}^{-2x}(x+3)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502983} {:answer/id #uuid "8f1283be-56ca-5686-943c-6ad9d8f99369" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int {e}^{-2x}(3x-1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503268} {:answer/id #uuid "97736758-b560-50cb-a82e-cff6127560fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int {e}^{-2x}(3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503280} {:answer/id #uuid "9cc67a8d-b547-5248-aabb-d2299c8fa6d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int {e}^{2x}(3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503294}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97736758-b560-50cb-a82e-cff6127560fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int {e}^{-2x}(3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503280} :answer-field/id #uuid "c479032f-a337-5fcb-94c4-f8162915858d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503613} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4334b854-8407-53fa-aaf6-9cf5934370e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2x}\\int {e}^{2x}(3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503110} {:answer/id #uuid "7eb41de7-3db6-5b13-bd58-a86afdf1107d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}\\int {e}^{-2x}(3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503243} {:answer/id #uuid "9a58fb98-9012-575d-a382-7233455d76fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2x}\\int {e}^{-2x}(x+3)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503288} {:answer/id #uuid "d0c274a9-0726-5088-99bc-154319b4baa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}\\int {e}^{-2x}(3x-1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503398}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7eb41de7-3db6-5b13-bd58-a86afdf1107d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}\\int {e}^{-2x}(3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503243} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8142952-f5ec-5c5e-80de-d77973e116bf" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ca23d1f9-e0ba-5c4f-a4c8-1fb3f4fded3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-337398" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y=3x+1.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{2x})}^{′}-2(u{e}^{2x}) & =3x+1 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2u{e}^{2x}-2u{e}^{2x} & =3x+1 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+\\cancel{2u{e}^{2x}}-\\cancel{2u{e}^{2x}} & =3x+1 \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x} & =3x+1 \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{-2x}(3x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{-2x}(3x+1) \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-2x}(3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\int {e}^{-2x}(3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{2x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{2x} \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{-2x}(3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {e}^{2x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\int {e}^{-2x}(3x+1)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478397 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157456 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ac34a18-053d-5eab-8c2c-bd0f15658186" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "497197b8-8e76-53b8-86a4-754349398785" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477880} {:answer/id #uuid "6b46c7a3-36fa-593b-b8a4-fd59ab9a5bab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477957} {:answer/id #uuid "9d326a77-95d4-5330-b18b-381ab039df29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478038} {:answer/id #uuid "ea6289c4-f3fa-56b7-a00e-e7a24c495194" 