meters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{4x})}^{′}-4(u{e}^{4x}) & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x}+4u{e}^{4x}-4u{e}^{4x} & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x}+\\cancel{4u{e}^{4x}}-\\cancel{4u{e}^{4x}} & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x} & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\int {e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{4x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{4x} \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {e}^{4x} \\\\  & ={e}^{4x}\\int {e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503723 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5fb547a-2518-5db9-b1b1-aab8d80328af" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using VOP When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1821fb92-1bc6-52cb-840b-fe52e847d9c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503031} {:answer/id #uuid "1e7ec567-b8e4-5e6b-b0e3-a54a500bf3c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503040} {:answer/id #uuid "a7422c58-6999-5683-9ddd-d6ef6add5c83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503319} {:answer/id #uuid "b4568322-688f-539c-8603-9923f672bd71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503350} {:answer/id #uuid "c0337af8-e9e5-53c9-b443-71e8a86df710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503365} {:answer/id #uuid "e6ee3d70-9cd7-5866-8013-20b03f25f1d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503456}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0337af8-e9e5-53c9-b443-71e8a86df710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503365} :answer-field/id #uuid "20ebff2a-086f-5eb7-8db3-ea98b1441d99" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-f