41e52db-7e65-579d-9821-9e969bdbaf23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397093} {:answer/id #uuid "104a2da1-9f11-510a-9e86-88dce1ddf0c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397094} {:answer/id #uuid "ffc049b9-0f4d-5a90-aa1e-01e88bb54d2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{15x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397095} {:answer/id #uuid "fe3e4f63-8e52-5102-b305-3e0c04fc80b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-10x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "104a2da1-9f11-510a-9e86-88dce1ddf0c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397094} :answer-field/id #uuid "23391fc0-0d97-510f-b68a-65d0d3b75416" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397091}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5bf23900-2f3d-53c0-9448-69b2b01a2a2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-337488" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5y=10\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{5x})}^{′}-5(u{e}^{5x}) & =10\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{5x}+5u{e}^{5x}-5u{e}^{5x} & =10\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{5x}+\\cancel{5u{e}^{5x}}-\\cancel{5u{e}^{5x}} & =10\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{5x} & =10\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =10{e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x=\\frac{{e}^{5x}+{e}^{-5x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =10{e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x \\\\  & =10{e}^{-5x}(\\frac{{e}^{5x}+{e}^{-5x}}{2}) \\\\  & =5{e}^{-5x}({e}^{5x}+{e}^{-5x}) \\\\  & =5+5{e}^{-10x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =5+5{e}^{-10x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 5+5{e}^{-10x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =5x-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-10x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{5x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{5x} \\\\  & =5x{e}^{5x}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5fb547a-2518-5db9-b1b1-aab8d80328af" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using VOP When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20068763-68a2-57b5-9b52-fcdbf1673fb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477796} {:answer/id #uuid "44976bea-1d15-5380-8acc-cf59a6c4390f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477864} {:answer/id #uuid "7b084f18-ccbb-5a85-8008-fa7d4b04c0ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477989} {:answer/id #uuid "ab44565a-5001-544b-a97e-e913f351705d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b084f18-ccbb-5a85-8008-fa7d4b04c0ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477989} :answer-field/id #uuid "0be86e77-5bbc-5a1a-8ae8-eba2439d5aa1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478249} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2159096d-027a-5318-a4e0-95ab68cc7864" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477798} {:answer/id #uuid "2271b0ca-5c98-5627-8d48-159512ffe466" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477803} {:answer/id #uuid "2f976a49-03c1-52ab-b068-a949d39698de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{6x}" :